"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[30309],{30309:n=>{n.exports="# SQL Functions — Date & Time\n\nRedshift date/time function → DBSQL mappings. For cross-cutting behavioral drift, see `references/redshift/00-quirks.md`.\n\n## REDSHIFT\n\n### Timestamp \"now\" functions\n\n| Redshift            | DBSQL               | Notes                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GETDATE()`         | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` |                                                                                                                       |\n| `SYSDATE`           | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` | `SYSDATE` is not a DBSQL keyword — must be replaced                                                                   |\n| `CURRENT_DATE`      | `CURRENT_DATE`      |                                                                                                                       |\n| `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` |                                                                                                                       |\n| `CURRENT_TIME`      | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` | DBSQL has no TIME type; carry timestamp or cast to STRING                                                             |\n| `TIMEOFDAY()`       | `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` | Return-type differs (string vs timestamp); wrap with `CAST(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP AS STRING)` if a string result is needed |\n\n### DATEADD\n\nRedshift: `DATEADD(unit, n, date)`. DBSQL: function depends on unit — arguments are always `(date, n)` style.\n\n| Redshift                      | DBSQL                                      |\n| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `DATEADD(day, n, date)`       | `DATE_ADD(date, n)`                        |\n| `DATEADD(week, n, date)`      | `DATE_ADD(date, n*7)`                      |\n| `DATEADD(month, n, date)`     | `ADD_MONTHS(date, n)`                      |\n| `DATEADD(quarter, n, date)`   | `ADD_MONTHS(date, n*3)`                    |\n| `DATEADD(year, n, date)`      | `ADD_MONTHS(date, n*12)`                   |\n| `DATEADD(hour, n, ts)`        | `ts + INTERVAL n HOURS`                    |\n| `DATEADD(minute, n, ts)`      | `ts + INTERVAL n MINUTES`                  |\n| `DATEADD(second, n, ts)`      | `ts + INTERVAL n SECONDS`                  |\n| `DATEADD(millisecond, n, ts)` | `ts + make_interval(0,0,0,0,0,0,n/1000.0)` |\n\n### DATEDIFF\n\nRedshift: `DATEDIFF(unit, start, end)` — counts **boundary crossings**.\n\nDBSQL has two forms:\n\n- `DATEDIFF(endDate, startDate)` — 2-arg, **days only**, returns INTEGER\n- `DATEDIFF(unit, start, end)` — 3-arg, any unit, synonym for `TIMESTAMPDIFF`, returns BIGINT. **Counts elapsed whole units**, not boundary crossings.\n\nThe 2-arg form has **reversed arguments** relative to Redshift. The 3-arg form has the **same argument order** but **different semantics** for partial units (elapsed vs boundary).\n\n| Redshift                     | DBSQL                                                 | Notes                                           |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `DATEDIFF(day, d1, d2)`      | `DATEDIFF(d2, d1)`                                    | **Arguments reversed; days only — no unit arg** |\n| `DATEDIFF(year, d1, d2)`     | `YEAR(d2) - YEAR(d1)`                                 | **Boundary crossing**                           |\n| `DATEDIFF(month, d1, d2)`    | `(YEAR(d2)*12+MONTH(d2)) - (YEAR(d1)*12+MONTH(d1))`   | **Boundary crossing**                           |\n| `DATEDIFF(quarter, d1, d2)`  | `(YEAR(d2)*4+QUARTER(d2)) - (YEAR(d1)*4+QUARTER(d1))` | **Boundary crossing**                           |\n| `DATEDIFF(hour, ts1, ts2)`   | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(HOUR, ts1, ts2)`                       | Same argument order; elapsed whole hours        |\n| `DATEDIFF(minute, ts1, ts2)` | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(MINUTE, ts1, ts2)`                     | Same argument order                             |\n| `DATEDIFF(second, ts1, ts2)` | `TIMESTAMPDIFF(SECOND, ts1, ts2)`                     | Same argument order                             |\n\n**When to pick which form**: If the source code relied on boundary-crossing semantics (e.g., `DATEDIFF(year, '2023-12-31', '2024-01-01') = 1`), use the arithmetic formula. If elapsed-unit semantics are acceptable, use `DATEDIFF(unit, ...)`.\n\n### DATE_TRUNC / DATE_PART / EXTRACT\n\nIdentical in both dialects — leave unchanged.\n\n### TRUNC(date)\n\n`TRUNC(datetime)` in Redshift truncates a timestamp to the date. In DBSQL, use `DATE(ts)` or `DATE_TRUNC('day', ts)`:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nSELECT TRUNC(order_ts) FROM orders\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT DATE(order_ts) FROM orders\n-- or\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('day', order_ts) FROM orders\n```\n\nNote: `TRUNC(numeric, n)` (numeric truncation) is identical in both dialects — no change needed.\n\n### LAST_DAY / MAKE_DATE / FORMAT\n\n| Redshift                   | DBSQL                      | Notes                                              |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `LAST_DAY(date)`           | `LAST_DAY(date)`           | Identical                                          |\n| `TO_DATE(str, 'fmt')`      | `TO_DATE(str, 'fmt')`      | Identical — but pattern syntax differs (see below) |\n| `TO_CHAR(ts, 'fmt')`       | `DATE_FORMAT(ts, 'fmt')`   | Pattern syntax differs — see below                 |\n| `TO_TIMESTAMP(str, 'fmt')` | `TO_TIMESTAMP(str, 'fmt')` | Same function, pattern syntax differs              |\n\n**Pattern differences** (PostgreSQL vs Java `SimpleDateFormat`):\n\n| Redshift (PG) | DBSQL (Java)     | Meaning                            |\n| ------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `YYYY`        | `yyyy`           | 4-digit year                       |\n| `MM`          | `MM`             | Month (both uppercase)             |\n| `DD`          | `dd`             | Day of month (Redshift uppercase)  |\n| `HH24`        | `HH`             | 24-hour                            |\n| `HH12`        | `hh`             | 12-hour                            |\n| `MI`          | `mm`             | Minutes (**DBSQL lowercase `mm`**) |\n| `SS`          | `ss`             | Seconds                            |\n| `MS` / `US`   | `SSS` / `SSSSSS` | Fractional seconds                 |\n| `AM` / `PM`   | `a`              | AM/PM marker                       |\n\n**Watch**: DBSQL uses lowercase `mm` for minutes; uppercase `MM` is month. A literal translation of a Redshift `HH24:MI:SS` → `HH:MM:SS` would format as hour:**month**:second, not hour:minute:second.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nSELECT TO_CHAR(ts, 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI:SS')\n\n-- DBSQL\nSELECT DATE_FORMAT(ts, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss')\n```\n\n### CONVERT_TIMEZONE\n\nRedshift: `CONVERT_TIMEZONE(src_tz, target_tz, ts)` (3-arg) or `CONVERT_TIMEZONE(target_tz, ts)` (2-arg, src assumed UTC).\n\nDBSQL has two 2-arg functions — pick based on intent:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift: convert from UTC to PST\nCONVERT_TIMEZONE('America/Los_Angeles', utc_ts)\n\n-- DBSQL: interpret as UTC, return local time in target zone\nFROM_UTC_TIMESTAMP(utc_ts, 'America/Los_Angeles')\n\n-- Redshift 3-arg: from PST to UTC\nCONVERT_TIMEZONE('America/Los_Angeles', 'UTC', local_ts)\n\n-- DBSQL: interpret local_ts as being in PST, convert to UTC\nTO_UTC_TIMESTAMP(local_ts, 'America/Los_Angeles')\n\n-- Redshift 3-arg general case: neither source nor target is UTC\nCONVERT_TIMEZONE('US/Eastern', 'US/Pacific', ts)\n\n-- DBSQL: chain through UTC\nFROM_UTC_TIMESTAMP(TO_UTC_TIMESTAMP(ts, 'US/Eastern'), 'US/Pacific')\n```\n\n### Day-of-week numbering\n\nRedshift `EXTRACT(DOW FROM date)`: 0=Sunday … 6=Saturday.\nDBSQL `DAYOFWEEK(date)`: 1=Sunday … 7=Saturday.\n\nAdjust by subtracting 1 if ordinal position matters:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nEXTRACT(DOW FROM order_date)\n\n-- DBSQL (equivalent numbering — match Redshift 0-indexed)\nDAYOFWEEK(order_date) - 1\n```\n\n### General scalar function mappings\n\n| Redshift                     | DBSQL                                       | Notes                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `NVL(x, y)`                  | `NVL(x, y)` or `COALESCE(x, y)`             | Identical                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `NVL2(x, y, z)`              | `NVL2(x, y, z)`                             | Native in DBSQL — identical semantics                                                                                                                       |\n| `DECODE(expr, s, r, ..., d)` | `DECODE(expr, s, r, ..., d)`                | Native in DBSQL with null-safe equality — matches Redshift                                                                                                  |\n| `LEN(str)`                   | `LENGTH(str)` or `LEN(str)`                 | `LEN` also works in DBSQL                                                                                                                                   |\n| `SPLIT_PART(str, sep, n)`    | `SPLIT_PART(str, sep, n)`                   | Identical                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `OCTET_LENGTH(str)`          | `OCTET_LENGTH(str)`                         | Identical                                                                                                                                                   |\n| `CHARINDEX(sub, str)`        | `CHARINDEX(sub, str)`                       | Native — no translation                                                                                                                                     |\n| `POSITION(sub IN str)`       | `POSITION(sub IN str)`                      | Native — no translation                                                                                                                                     |\n| `STRPOS(str, sub)`           | `INSTR(str, sub)` or `POSITION(sub IN str)` | DBSQL has no `STRPOS`. `INSTR` is a drop-in replacement (same `(str, sub)` arg order); `POSITION` is also native but takes the inverted `(sub IN str)` form |\n\n### Session and catalog functions (leader-node-only)\n\nRedshift has a set of functions that run only on the leader node. Some have direct DBSQL equivalents; others do not.\n\n**Direct replacements** (session / catalog info):\n\n| Redshift           | DBSQL               | Notes                                     |\n| ------------------ | ------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `CURRENT_USER`     | `current_user()`    | Same semantics — current session username |\n| `CURRENT_DATABASE` | `current_catalog()` | Redshift database ↔ DBSQL Unity Catalog   |\n| `CURRENT_SCHEMA()` | `current_schema()`  | Same                                      |\n| `SESSION_USER`     | `session_user()`    | Same                                      |\n\n**No equivalent** — remove or TODO:\n\n- `CURRENT_USER_ID` — Redshift-specific integer user ID; no direct DBSQL counterpart.\n- `STV_*`, `STL_*`, `SVV_*`, `SVCS_*`, `SVL_*` system tables — no direct equivalent.\n- `PG_*` catalog views — partial coverage in `information_schema.*`; prefer UC system tables (`system.information_schema.*`) when replacing catalog lookups.\n\n### Hash functions\n\n| Redshift            | DBSQL              | Notes                                                                                   |\n| ------------------- | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `MD5(str)`          | `MD5(str)`         | Identical                                                                               |\n| `SHA1(str)`         | `SHA1(str)`        | Identical                                                                               |\n| `SHA256(str)`       | `SHA2(str, 256)`   | DBSQL takes bit-length as second argument                                               |\n| `SHA512(str)`       | `SHA2(str, 512)`   | DBSQL takes bit-length as second argument                                               |\n| `FUNC_SHA1` (alias) | `SHA1(str)`        | Use canonical name                                                                      |\n| `CHECKSUM(...)`     | No direct analogue | Hash inputs with `MD5` and combine, or emit a TODO if exact CHECKSUM bytes are required |\n\n### Time-zone arithmetic\n\n`AT TIME ZONE` is supported in DBSQL with the same operator syntax. For explicit UTC ↔ session conversions, prefer `from_utc_timestamp(ts, tz)` and `to_utc_timestamp(ts, tz)`:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nSELECT ts AT TIME ZONE 'UTC' FROM events;\n\n-- DBSQL — same operator works; or use the function form\nSELECT ts AT TIME ZONE 'UTC' FROM my_catalog.my_schema.events;\nSELECT from_utc_timestamp(ts, 'UTC') FROM my_catalog.my_schema.events;\n```\n"}}]);
